Lithium reacts vigorously with water to form [ lithium hydroxide
](/wiki/Lithium_hydroxide "Lithium hydroxide") (LiOH) and [ hydrogen
](/wiki/Hydrogen "Hydrogen") gas. Thus, a non-aqueous electrolyte is typically
used, and a sealed container rigidly excludes moisture from the battery pack.
The non-aqueous electrolyte is typically a mixture of organic carbonates such
as [ ethylene carbonate ](/wiki/Ethylene_carbonate "Ethylene carbonate") and [
propylene carbonate ](/wiki/Propylene_carbonate "Propylene carbonate")
containing [ complexes ](/wiki/Coordination_complex "Coordination complex") of
lithium ions.  [51]  [ Ethylene carbonate ](/wiki/Ethylene_carbonate "Ethylene
carbonate") is essential for making solid electrolyte interphase on the carbon
anode,  [52]  but since it is solid at room temperature, a liquid [ solvent
](/wiki/Solvent "Solvent") (such as [ propylene carbonate
](/wiki/Propylene_carbonate "Propylene carbonate") or [ diethyl carbonate
](/wiki/Diethyl_carbonate "Diethyl carbonate") ) is added.

