The making of  [ distilled spirits
](https://www.britannica.com/topic/distilled-spirit) begins with the mashes of
grains, fruits, or other ingredients. The resultant fermented liquid is heated
until the alcohol and flavourings vaporize and can be drawn off, cooled, and
condensed back into a liquid. Water remains behind and is [ discarded
](https://www.britannica.com/dictionary/discarded) . The concentrated liquid,
called a distilled beverage, includes such liquors as [ whiskey
](https://www.britannica.com/topic/whiskey) , [ gin
](https://www.britannica.com/topic/gin-liquor) , [ vodka
](https://www.britannica.com/topic/vodka) , [ rum
](https://www.britannica.com/topic/rum-liquor) , brandy, and liqueurs, or
cordials. They range in alcoholic content usually from 40 to 50 percent,
though higher or lower concentrations are found.

